WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.960
Agents may receive instructions from developers, users, system prompts, retrieved documents, web pages, emails, tickets, files, tool responses, or other agents.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:16.340
The application may intend these sources to have different authority, but the model sees natural language from all of them.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.160
Untrusted content can therefore resemble an instruction even when it was meant to be treated only as data.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:28.060
Prompt injection is the high-level risk that input changes model behavior in an unintended way.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.840
Direct injection comes from a user interaction.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.860
Indirect injection comes from external content that the agent later reads or retrieves.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.200
The defensive lesson is not that every document is hostile.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:46.140
It is that external content should not be allowed to grant itself authority over permissions, policies, or high-impact actions.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:54.120
Instruction hierarchy and clear context can help the model follow the intended task, but wording alone is not a reliable security boundary.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:58.580
Authorization must be enforced by application code and identity systems.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.400
The model should not be able to expand its own permissions, reveal protected data, or approve a sensitive action merely because text asked it to do so.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:18.460
Safer designs separate trusted configuration from untrusted content, preserve source and provenance, validate tool arguments, constrain downstream actions, and require approval where impact is high.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:24.040
Teams should test how the agent behaves when content is confusing, contradictory, or misleading.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:30.020
If instruction handling fails, surrounding controls should still prevent unauthorized access or execution.
